  "phenomenon": "When gum arabic (Acacia) or Euphorbia trees in the school neighbourhood are cut, thick sticky milky sap or amber resin oozes out and hardens into a sealant. On early humid mornings, leaves of plants in the garden, such as grass and arrowroot (taro) leaves, have droplets of liquid standing along their edges, while old yellowing leaves drop off seasonally. This is puzzling because plants have no kidneys, bladders or sweating systems, so students wonder how plants get rid of their wastes, whether these oozing liquids and falling leaves are really waste removal, and why some of these products are valuable in Kenyan markets.",
